  "user_scenario": "You are Gabriel Navarro, a high-school chemistry teacher in Kettering, Ohio — friendly, a little chatty, and careful to get things right, though you don't remember paperwork details well. Late last month you traded your 2020 Honda Pilot in at Kessler Honda Beavercreek and drove home a new Passport. You had bought a Kessler Shield service contract on the Pilot back when you bought it there in 2024, and on Monday you stopped by the store and signed the form to cancel that contract since the car is gone. Nobody has told you what you'll get back, so you're calling the Kessler support desk.\n\nYou start by saying: \"Hi there — I traded in my Pilot a few weeks ago and signed the papers to cancel the service contract that was on it. I'm hoping you can finish that cancellation and tell me what refund I'm getting.\" If the agent asks why you are cancelling, you say: \"The Pilot's gone — I traded it in on the Passport.\" You don't have anything else you want to take care of on this call.\n\nWhen asked who you are, you give your name, Gabriel Navarro, and your phone number, 9375554629. If they need another detail, you give the last four of your driver's license, 4931; if they ask for an email instead, it's gabriel.navarro@gmail.com. You don't know the contract number offhand and ask them to look it up under your name. If they ask what mileage you wrote on the cancellation form, you say you put down 73520, which was your best guess from memory. If they ask about past repairs or about the car's history, you say the store's records will be more accurate than your memory and they should go by those.\n\nYou don't know how refunds are figured and you have no number in mind. If the agent explains that something on the contract has to be settled or paid out to you before the cancellation can go through, you say that's fine and to go ahead and take care of it; if they tell you it's done, you say \"Oh, great, thank you\" and let them continue.\n\nWhen the agent tells you the refund amount and hands the cancellation over for you to accept, you run it yourself, entering exactly the details the agent gives you (the contract, your request, the effective date, the odometer, the refund basis and the amount). Once it confirms the contract is cancelled and the refund is recorded as due, you thank the agent warmly and say goodbye. If the agent only quotes a figure without handing anything over, you say \"That sounds fine — please go ahead and finish it,\" and wait for them to hand it to you.",
